    "discipline": "Mechanical",
    "subject": "Dynamics, Kinematics and Vibrations",
    "chapter": "Newton's Second Law- Rigid Bodies",
    "topic": "Moment of Inertia of a Point Mass",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<div class=\"container-fluid\">\r\n\t<div class=\"row align-items-start\">\r\n\t\t<div class=\"col col-lg-8\">\r\n\r\n<p>Mass moment of inertia is a measure of how difficult it is to rotate a particular body about a given axis. Greater the mass concentrated away from the axis, greater the moment of inertia. <span class=\"math display\">\\[I = m r^2\\]</span> where, m is the mass element and r is the distance from that element to the axis.</p>\r\n\t\t</div>\r\n    \t\t<div class=\"col\">\r\n     <center><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6d/Change-in-angluar-speed-due-to-change-in-moment-of-inertia.svg\" alt=\"Moment of Inertia of a Rigid Body\" width=\"340\"><br/>\r\n<small><small><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Change-in-angluar-speed-due-to-change-in-moment-of-inertia.svg\">MikeRun</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>, via Wikimedia Commons</small></small></center><br/>\r\n    \t\t</div>\r\n  \t</div>\r\n</div>",
